{{Use dmy dates|date=July 2025}} {{short description|The history of the institution of chattel slavery, resistance, and abolition in Balisca.}} {{about|the history of slavery in Balisca|the slave trade in the Abyala|Mesogean colonization of Abyala#Slavery}}
[[File:Rodolfo Amoedo - Ciclo do Ouro, Acervo do Museu Paulista da USP.jpg|thumb|''Ciclo d'o Oro'' ("The Gold Cycle"), depicting enslaved Kaftians and natives during the [[Bayaran gold rush]]]]
The history of '''slavery in Balisca''' is a violent and complex chapter, defined by its early and substantial reliance on the exploitation of [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous peoples]], and later on the [[Aethiopian slave trade]], its unique ideological divisions over the practice, and an intense, successful resistance that led to an early, principled abolition. Although the practice was ''de jure'' outlawed in 1809, the [[Confederal Question|struggle]] over its economic and political legacy became the central catalyst for the [[Baliscan Civil War]] (1828–1838).

During the initial competition between the various Illypnian expeditions for control of the [[Northern Triangle]], their colonists were heavily dependent on indigenous labor to maintain their [[W:Subsistence economy|subsistence economy]], in which natives were often captured by expeditions of [[W:Bandeirantes|''bandeirantes'']]. The [[Reapers' War]] led to a decline in the practice of enslavement of natives by the Xacano colonists, leading to the adoption of [[W:Chattel slavery|chattel slavery]] to satisfy their growing demand for labor. The first ship carrying [[Kaftia|Kaftian]] slaves arrived in Balisca in 1533 at [[Corpus Christi]]. 

Historically, an estimated 60% of enslaved people brought to Balisca from West/Central Kaftia were Muslims, who were often literate and viewed as potentially dangerous by Mesogean colonial authorities. The merchant republics and later the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|Volisanian Crown]], including the [[Hesperian League]], willingly participated in the Aethiopian slave trade to fuel their economic interests. Unlike the Volisanian model, however, the early [[W:Factory (trading post)|merchant factories]] were subject to the [[Abyala Charter]] (post-1537), which prohibited expansion into the interior of [[Pindorama]], in an attempt to delineate territory and minimize further settler conflicts. Furthermore, the [[Catharism|Cathar communities]] operating in the region actively strongly condemned the forcing of natives into chattel slavery across the territory, adding an immediate moral and religious division to the colonial society.

The abolition of slavery and the destruction of the slave-owning class was [[Granyana Convention|part of the ideology]] of the [[Northern Confederation|republican/nationalist forces]] during the [[Continuation War]], and the defense of the institution of slavery was a defining characteristic of the Bayaran economic elite that would align with the [[Volisania|centralized regime in Volisania]]. Nationalist forces would emerge victorious following the [[Battle of Navassa]], and the last enslaved peoples were emancipated in 1818, bringing an end to almost 300 years of slavery in the territories of Balisca.

== History ==

=== Opposition and slave revolts ===
{{Further|Maroon Wars|Maroon pirates|Maroon Confederacy}}

=== Abolition and continued unrest ===
{{Main|Continuation War|Confederal Question}}

[[File:Capitao-mato.jpg|left|thumb|334x334px|''Cuarenta acres y una mula'' {{Circa|1823}}, depicting newly emancipated [[Kafto-Baliscans|Kafto-Bayaran]] landowners in [[Macaio Province]]]]
Immediately following the [[Treaty of Navassa|securing of independence]], dissatisfaction with the confederal system of governance emerged. The republics began to look to their own interests rather than the confederation. During the war, confederal forces had imprisoned, hanged, or expelled all former slave owners (most relocated to [[Potocsí]] and [[Sanrobonia]]), and divided their land and other assets between the newly emancipated Kaftian population. This further hardened attitudes in Bayara against confederation, which already was strong due to the devastation in Bayara in terms of infrastructure and economy. Because of the reluctance of the land-owning class accept the loss of their privileges and to grant voting rights to freedmen, the [[Confederal Congress]] adopted [[Reconstruction laws]] over Bayaran objections. In response, and intending to avoid the social effects of the war, most of the Bayaran provinces passed [[W:Black codes|black codes]]. These were mostly ''de jure'' nullified by confederal law and pro-reformist legislatures, but widely continued to be enforced by Bayaran officials.

This [[Confederal Question|constitutional crisis]] was amplified by [[Pastry War|ethnoreligious conflict]] in the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]], which ultimately led to the [[Treaty of Navassa (1820)|1820 Treaty of Navassa]] and the ''de facto'' partition of a predominately Volisanian-speaking republic from Bayara. The crisis peaked when the [[Compromise of 1827]], which proposed strengthening the central government, was rejected and ignored by Bayaran delegates. Convinced that their former privileges were permanently lost, the [[General Junta (Bayara)|General Junta]] denounced the Articles of Confederation on 6 July 1828, and the secessionist [[Xunta Nacionalista]] mobilized for war.

==== Maroon removal policy ====
{{Main|Maroon resettlement|Basilio mutiny}}

Forced displacement of communities of natives and freedmen from the territory of Bayara became official policy of the Bayaran secessionist [[Xunta Nacionalista]] following its denunciation of the [[Articles of Confederation]] in 1828. The policy is considered a consequence of actions first by the [[Reapers' War|Illypnian colonists]] and then later on by the [[W:Bandeirantes|Bayaran settlers]] seeking to exploit the unexplored interior of [[Abyala]] during the [[Colonial Balisca|colonial era]] and then through the [[Maroon Wars]]. Removal policies had earlier gained popularity as tensions festered within Bayaran society regarding the [[W:Abolition of slavery|abolition of slavery]] by the [[Northern Confederation]] via the 1809 [[Granyana Convention]].

After the official adoption of the policy by the Xunta, approximately 150,000 [[W:Freedmen|freedmen]] and [[W:Free persons of color|free persons of color]], and 60,000 members of the [[Honú-Abá]], [[Murí people|Murí]] (''costeños''), [[Aruanãyará]], [[Roáka'á]], and [[Kalútupí]] [[Indigenous nations in Balisca|nations]] (which earlier resisted displacement from their ancestral homelands), were expelled from ''quilombos'' in Bayara to the [[Maroon Confederacy]]. Growing support in Bayara for the forced resettlement intensified the [[Confederal Question|confederal crises]] of the post-independence years, ultimately leading to the [[Basilio mutiny]] and the subsequent [[Baliscan Civil War]]. The resettlement policy was revoked in 1838 with the [[Treaty of Bixessarri]], and the new [[Anyos Federales|Reconstruction]] government facilitated the [[W:Right of return|right to return]] for the displaced, along with reparations (in the form of [[W:Land redistribution|land redistribution]]), the recognition of indigenous sovereignty, and constitutional guarantees of cultural and political rights for all communities, with aggressive confederal protection.

